INT. HOTEL BALLROOM - ARBITRATION HEARING - DAY

A chandeliered ballroom pressed into service. A makeshift bench of two folding tables. Through the folding partition, the thump of a wedding band warming up next door.

PRUDENCE OKEKE-LINDGREN (40s, navy suit, steady, a legal pad crowded with her own handwriting) stands at a lectern. Across the aisle, GARRICK SOAMES (50s, pinstripes, silver tie clip, a smile like a trap) sits sprawled, one ankle across his knee. The arbitrator's chair is empty; the arbitrator has gone to find a stapler.

PRUDENCE
Mr. Soames, while we wait.

GARRICK
Ms. Okeke-Lindgren, always a pleasure.

PRUDENCE
I wanted to ask about the Kowalski contract.

GARRICK
I can't imagine what there is to ask.

PRUDENCE
Page nine. Clause fourteen.

GARRICK
Boilerplate.

PRUDENCE
It says your client "shall deliver all materials by the thirty-first."

GARRICK
It does.

PRUDENCE
The thirty-first of what?

Garrick's smile does not move. He straightens his tie clip.

GARRICK
I believe it's clear from context.

PRUDENCE
Is it? Because my client received a delivery on the first of the following month. And your client's position is that this was timely.

GARRICK
It was timely.

PRUDENCE
By one day.

GARRICK
By one day, yes.

Prudence opens her folder and lays a single sheet on the lectern, smoothing it flat with the heel of her hand. Garrick watches her fingers, then the page, then her face.

PRUDENCE
This is a calendar. Of the month in question.

GARRICK
I don't need a calendar.

PRUDENCE
Humor me.

She slides it across the aisle. Garrick does not touch it. She waits. The wedding band, next door, begins a slow waltz. Garrick finally reaches for the page, holds it at arm's length, then draws it closer, reading the tiny numerals with growing attention.

GARRICK
There is no thirty-first.

PRUDENCE
There is not.

GARRICK
The month has thirty days.

PRUDENCE
It does.

GARRICK
(carefully)
Then the clause is ambiguous.

PRUDENCE
It is. And under the doctrine of contra proferentem, ambiguity is construed against the drafter.

GARRICK
Which was my client.

PRUDENCE
Which was your client.

Garrick drops the calendar. It flutters to the carpet. He stands, buttons his jacket, and walks to the lectern in two long strides, his shadow falling across her pad.

GARRICK
Prudence.

PRUDENCE
Garrick.

GARRICK
You've done your homework.

PRUDENCE
I always do.

GARRICK
What do you want?

PRUDENCE
Performance. Delivery of the materials, with interest, and costs.

GARRICK
My client will never agree.

PRUDENCE
Then we will let the arbitrator decide, as we agreed to do.

The partition shakes as the wedding band crashes into its first full chorus. Garrick flinches. Prudence does not. She gathers her pad, taps it into alignment, and sets it down.

GARRICK
(low)
You have been planning this for months.

PRUDENCE
For eleven weeks. I have been quiet.

GARRICK
It is not like you.

PRUDENCE
It's exactly like me.

A door opens at the far end. The ARBITRATOR is not seen, only the sound of a stapler on a folding table. Garrick glances over his shoulder, then back.

GARRICK
Fifty thousand.

PRUDENCE
Eighty.

GARRICK
Sixty.

PRUDENCE
Eighty-five. And you pay for the ballroom.

GARRICK
Why would I pay for the ballroom?

PRUDENCE
Because the wedding next door is mine.

He stares. The band erupts into "Congratulations." Prudence smiles for the first time, small, private, and tilts her head toward the music.

PRUDENCE (CONT'D)
My daughter's.

Garrick closes his eyes. His shoulders drop. When he opens them, he is almost laughing.

GARRICK
Eighty-five.

PRUDENCE
Done.

She holds out her hand. He takes it. They shake, once, hard.

GARRICK
You know you're insufferable.

PRUDENCE
And yet.

She gathers her things and walks briskly toward the partition. Garrick watches her go, then stoops, picks up the calendar, and tucks it into his pocket like a trophy.
